 Elk Output View` on the view -- and so selected whatever\nnode the layout had put there. When the diagram sat at the origin instead (a\nfrontend race, also seen on #162) nothing was selected and the gate failed.\nThose clicks are gone: the toolbar shows on `:hover` and needed no click, and\nSprotty ignores the right button, so the context menu needed none either.\n03_App now selects n1 deliberately (`Select Elk Node`: a pointer click on the\nnode''s label, then Sprotty''s `selected` class) and waits for the kernel''s\ncontrol-overlay button, so the round trip is asserted rather than hoped for.\nChecked with the full suite locally: 16/16 pass and the selection code runs in\n03_App and in no other suite.\n\nThe deliberate selection runs only where coverage is measured. On the `oldest`\nfrontend (JupyterLab 4.1.8, ipywidgets 8.0.1) selecting a node runs Firefox\nout of memory within seconds, on all three platforms: CI artifacts show the\nbrowser dying right after the first click, and the same accidental selection\nis what took `test-oldest (ubuntu-latest)` to GitHub''s 6-hour limit twice on\nmaster in one day (02_Transformer, right after the view''s centre click). That\nis a real defect for users on old frontends and is filed separately; removing\nthe accidental clicks stops it from hanging CI.\n\nBound every notebook suite to ten minutes. When the browser dies, geckodriver\nstops answering and the teardown spends ~10 minutes per Selenium call on\nurllib3 retries; Robot''s `Test Timeout` does not cover teardown. pabot runs one\nnotebook suite per process; `--processtimeout 600` kills a suite (setup, test\nand teardown) that overruns, with no orphaned browser or driver left behind\n(checked locally with a 20-second timeout). `Test Timeout` drops to 8 minutes\nso a hung test still fails as a Robot failure, with its screenshot, before the\nprocess is killed; the longest wait inside a test is 5 minutes. The CI step\nkeeps a 45-minute wall clock as the outer belt. Two waits that a loaded macOS\nrunner (four browsers on three cores) kept missing are retried or longer: the\nfile browser is refreshed until it lists a just-copied notebook (five of nine\nfailures in one macOS run were this), and the control-overlay button gets 30 s.\n\nMake the headless kernel wedge diagnosable. `itest-oldest-nbconvert` fails\nabout one run in five, across branches, always the same way: `03_App.ipynb`,\nthe cell after `display(box)`, 1200 s, the kernel silent -- a `def` cell that\nthe kernel never answered because something the previous cell left running\nstarved its event loop. Nobody has seen the kernel''s stack at that moment, and\nan equivalent environment did not reproduce it locally. The kernels nbconvert\nlaunches now run `scripts/nbconvert_kernel_watchdog.py` as an IPython startup\nfile (`IPYTHONDIR`, set by the `nbconvert--` task): `faulthandler` dumps every\nthread''s stack to the kernel''s original stderr after 900 s, which ipykernel\nforwards to the job log. It needs neither the GIL nor the event loop, so a\nbusy loop cannot hide from it; healthy kernels exit in seconds and never\nreach it. Checked with a 0.5 s timer: the dump lands in nbconvert''s stderr,\nnot in any cell output, and conversion still succeeds.\n\nAlso fixes a pre-existing race in `test_schedule_run_from_another_loop_hands_over`\n(it failed on windows/py3.10 in this PR''s CI and passed on retry): the test\nreleased the parked runs as soon as the old runner was cancelled, but the new\nrunner may not have parked yet, and a run that parks after `release` waits\nforever. The poll now also waits for the new run to have started.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqwYi8CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAb+4QAJpraWMAHyRRo60WXWrs/T9Q\nyv4sNy+cpm7CkqShzM5Iy8CdXgC54ZJvTZLib3xDj/AbuQTmmDL2Dc0G+ZrMMV6d\nYxdGn+C5m2U4RPsj6iWDfHHyias9dp9KkDhC6c3KLhqQRTkhHGWuFuwH8mXqP14F\ngMR2YkXEPjvV0ywknkRJWgVCIoOTDNKtLUNauIofsQlthwn7dBGSBLqwM+0lMlpW\nMYwTB/OApfcSmam0tKLgcdKwxmmq0u6q