}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/sanbales/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/sanbales","id":1438114,"login":"sanbales","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzgxMTQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/sanbales/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/sanbales/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/sanbales/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/sanbales/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/sanbales/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/sanbales","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyrdf/ipyelk/commits/ad904ce5717036950f3c497a40b5f3b83163a9dd/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-01T01:13:36Z","email":"sanbales@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Santiago Balestrini-Robinson"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-01T01:13:36Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: introduce the 3.0 tool API (#157)","tree":{"sha":"f42528c3a59a4255e9a33789cc70325e29ee3dca","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyrdf/ipyelk/git/trees/f42528c3a59a4255e9a33789cc70325e29ee3dca"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyrdf/ipyelk/git/commits/ad904ce5717036950f3c497a40b5f3b83163a9dd","verification":{"payload":"tree f42528c3a59a4255e9a33789cc70325e29ee3dca\nparent 54fd52eb288f1eedf2c1e903900b6dde9855995c\nauthor Santiago Balestrini-Robinson <sanbales@users.noreply.github.com> 1790817216 -0400\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790817216 -0400\n\nfeat: introduce the 3.0 tool API (#157)","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqvbPACRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAPPcQADvG5DuRq6Ripc6BBJsaRshm\n57/qQ0A/o6JWhv7oe2+UYlqbHNhpQzUep7pNzMXK39mFQBGK12eE9KmDRdMCzm/w\npZpDbnB9tvaXfn+3F+CpLuTZfZZU4ulyhtVQqSjG7aezN6a3kMD2ruJ00PQcms4i\n1mFTQ00aW/k8X/Ph6TH8MO4KVxPQ9l9IQlOf1dQxpVjyX5cPVqQDNi0WG30CBbgf\n/mGLxBE8jbFj2GdDiWyb6HJ3YbU7jaJ9cPovBpky8qM2W/olLvaD+VD2vjYOXeZQ\nZkHXO2cAkAajTHLQlyHo/46t6oQDvqbRGqs6reProW56T4juMki9TSlMhDgd8IWR\nIR6HfFzkVsHfq9NxYjDykmooG6xCrjeEiTDbQyWBVnDSRKUk/DPwlxEXTjphFmcp\n35wIZM2HA58tjDIh/I4Lp7ugU+cd/C5l6Rl0yMh2emiG7E8jIT3+MRXLGxowRfaR\nD4WyTRGH5Ze4Wted1uZauACh5wgrmYpW9GA/hkyfhWSk31r0CTliaeXt5i9Yxwv4\nPRRrQ0w6YBOeIzBPROTnNmkFpQlNodtim0aqROasiieUDxMcZe04E0ErqiSR6wO3\njFYj+z6ZFD/p1Zb8ER2m2c45Ae8tnj85OENTqrfwE4go7X9a4EGWvnbSBKMJMLHD\nuPO5h5uVP0FecXib3CPe\n=5WAC\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-01T01:13:36Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/jupyrdf/ipyelk/commit/ad904ce5717036950f3c497a40b5f3b83163a9dd","node_id":"C_kwDOEQFCU9oAKGFkOTA0Y2U1NzE3MDM2OTUwZjNjNDk3YTQwYjVmM2I4MzE2M2E5ZGQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/jupyrdf/ipyelk/commit/54fd52eb288f1eedf2c1e903900b6dde9855995c","sha":"54fd52eb288f1eedf2c1e903900b6dde9855995c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyrdf/ipyelk/commits/54fd52eb288f1eedf2c1e903900b6dde9855995c"}],"sha":"ad904ce5717036950f3c497a40b5f3b83163a9dd","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyrdf/ipyelk/commits/ad904ce5717036950f3c497a40b5f3b83163a9dd"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/6493354?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dfreeman06/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dfreeman06/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dfreeman06/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dfreeman06/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/dfreeman06","id":6493354,"login":"dfreeman06","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY0OTMzNTQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dfreeman06/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dfreeman06/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dfreeman06/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dfreeman06/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dfreeman06/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dfreeman06","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyrdf/ipyelk/commits/54fd52eb288f1eedf2c1e903900b6dde9855995c/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-01T00:10:17Z","email":"freeman.dane@gmail.com","name":"dfreeman06"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-01T00:10:17Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"2.1.x substrate patch: echo off, take-at-start flow, generation counter, no inlet swap, linear validation (#168)\n\n* Add headless pipeline benchmark harness\n\nscripts/bench_pipeline.py runs a Diagram refresh without a browser, stubs\nthe text sizer (fixed width per character) and runs real elkjs in node,\nfeeds results back through the kernel''s receive path so echoes are\ncounted, and records wall time, per-stage time, comm messages by kind,\nbytes on the wire, layout runs, a collapse refresh, and a ten-refresh\nburst. scripts/bench_graphs.py generates deterministic flat and nested\ngraphs at 100/500/1000 nodes.\n\nSingle command: pixi run -e utest python scripts/bench_pipeline.py --label master\n\nRefs #164, #161, #167\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Stop echoing browser-written traits back to the frontends\n\nSelection.ids, Hover.ids and MarkElementWidget.value are written by the\nbrowser. With ipywidgets'' default echo_update the kernel re-sent each\nbrowser write to every frontend, and for value it then sent a second\nupdate carrying the re-serialised pydantic tree (which differs from the\nbrowser JSON by elkjs-internal keys), so every browser result arrived\nback twice and the viewer re-rendered on each. Tag the three traits\necho_update=False and, for value, skip the second send while the\nbrowser''s property lock is held. Kernel-initiated writes still send once.\n\nKnown limitation, documented on the traits: a second frontend attached\nto the same kernel no longer learns browser-made selections through the\necho (#161 rebroadcast question; a decision for the RenderBridge).\n\nRefs #164 (item 1), #161 defect 2\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Make edge-owner validation linear in the hierarchy\n\nElementIndex.check_edges asked networkx for the lowest common ancestor\nof every edge''s endpoints, and that call re-verifies the whole hierarchy\nis acyclic each time, so validation cost edges times nodes and ran twice\nper ValidationPipe.run. Compute depths once per check with a stack walk\nover children and find owners with a parent walk (ports resolve to their\nnode, self-loops to the node''s parent). ValidationPipe.apply_fixes now\nreports how many fixes it made and run() only re-reports on the outlet\nwhen something changed.\n\nMeasured on 10 groups with 3 root-appended edges per node:\n\n  nodes  edges  before    after\n    100    300  0.146 s   0.001 s\n    500   1500  3.533 s   0.005 s\n   1000   3000  14.420 s  0.010 s\n\nOwners are identical to networkx''s on seeded random trees (parametrized\ntest), including orphan adoption, port endpoints, and self-loops; the\npre-existing raise for a self-loop on a parentless node is preserved and\npinned.\n\nRefs #164 (item 5), #161 defect 1\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Take the pending flow at run start and stop swapping the inlet\n\nWriters recorded pending reports by assigning inlet.flow, and the\npipeline cleared it by assigning () when a run completed, so a report\nrecorded while a run was in flight (a tool that failed after mutating\nthe model, or a style change between completion and its callback) was\nerased by that completion. MarkElementWidget gains record(*tags), an\norder-preserving union, and take(), which returns the pending flow and\nleaves () behind. Pipeline.run takes at start and re-records what it\ntook if the run fails or is cancelled; Diagram, Tool.handler and\nToggleCollapsedTool record instead of assigning; Diagram.update_view no\nlonger clears the flow.\n\nDiagram.refresh also stopped swapping the browser''s laid-out copy into\npipe.inlet.value. The swap carried nothing that outlet.persist() does\nnot already merge onto the user''s objects through the shared index, and\non the next New run (a style change, for instance) validation rebuilt\nthe index from that copy, so selection resolved to copies and a hidden\nnode resolved to the slack Port sharing its id. ValidationPipe now\nrecognises a swapped-in merged copy and validates the indexed root.\n\nRefs #164 (items 2 and 4), #160 defect 3, #161\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Serve refresh requests with a generation counter instead of cancelling\n\nPipe.schedule_run cancelled the in-flight task and started another, so\na burst of refreshes cancelled runs the browser was already laying out,\na cancelled run''s browser answer could be consumed by its successor, and\nthe resend loop (every 0.5 to 2 s until answered) queued a duplicate\nlayout in the worker for every re-sent request.\n\nschedule_run now only counts a request. One runner task serves requests\non the trailing edge (ten calls in one tick cost one run; a call mid-run\ncosts one more run after it). A Pipeline gives way to a newer request at\nits next stage boundary (Superseded, taken flow re-recorded), and a\nbrowser roundtrip that was sent is always awaited. Pipe.cancel() is the\nexplicit way to stop a run; Diagram uses it only when its pipe or source\nis replaced. Each roundtrip carries a generation in the run message and\nthe frontend writes it back with its answer (MarkElementWidget.gen), so\nan answer for an abandoned generation is ignored and a re-sent request\nis recognised as the same work: the frontend RunQueue lays out once per\ngeneration and queues at most one newer request. Answers without a\ngeneration (an older extension build) are accepted with a one-time\nwarning.\n\nMeasured with the harness''s --slow-browser 0.6 at 100 nodes: layouts per\nrefresh 2 -> 1, wall 1.48 s -> 0.78 s. Immediate-answer path unchanged.\n\nRefs #164 (item 3), #161 defects 3 and 8\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Resolve roundtrips on the answer''s generation, not on a value change\n\nReview of the generation counter found a regression: the browser''s\noutlet update is Backbone''s deep-equal diff, so a re-run whose layout is\nidentical to what the frontend model already holds arrives as a change\nof gen alone (or as nothing at all on a third identical run), and\nwait_for_answer, which observed only value, waited out the 30 s\ntimeout. Before this branch the kernel''s re-serialised update after\nevery browser write kept the frontend value from ever being deep-equal,\nan accidental nonce that the echo suppression removed.\n\nwait_for_answer now observes value and gen and resolves when the\noutlet''s gen matches, with outlet.value as the result. The frontend\nanswers through one shared helper that silently clears value before the\nreal set so the tree is always in the diff too, for older kernels.\nError reports carry the failed request''s generation and the kernel\nrejects the pending roundtrip only for a matching generation (or when\nthe report has none). The gen-0 acceptance for older frontends now\nrequires the write to come from the browser (property lock held), so a\nkernel-side write during the first roundtrip is not taken for an answer.\nThe frontend RunQueue also ignores a re-sent request for a generation it\nalready completed, and the text sizer measures on a 250 ms timer when\nrequestAnimationFrame does not fire (background tab).\n\nThe harness emulates the frontend''s diff so this path is exercised.\n\nRefs #164 (item 3)\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Address review findings on the substrate patch\n\nEcho suppression: MarkElementWidget now suppresses the re-send only for\nthe very object the browser wrote (captured in set_trait while the\nproperty lock is held), so an observer that assigns a new tree during a\nbrowser write is still sent once.\n\nFlow: a superseded run that swallows its cancellation and completes late\nno longer resets the successor''s taken flow; a Pipeline nested as a\nsub-pipe receives the flow from its parent instead of re-taking from the\nshared inlet (and no longer re-records on failure, since the parent owes\nit); the ValidationPipe guard for a swapped-in browser copy is removed,\nsince nothing swaps the inlet any more and the guard could discard a\nuser''s edited tree.\n\nRunner: a cancelled roundtrip only clears the pipe''s roundtrip future if\nit still owns it (Python 3.10/3.11 unwind a turn late); the runner yields\nbetween runs so a request made from inside a synchronous run cannot spin\nthe loop; the older-frontend warning is per pipe; docstrings state that\non_error and status reflect the runner''s final outcome, that ownership\nvalidation trusts parent links, and that a source widget should feed one\npipeline.\n\nTests: record-not-assign is pinned at every call site (two tools mid-run,\nthe collapse tool, Diagram''s source flow); the late-unwind interleavings\nare reproduced with a pipe that catches CancelledError and yields; LCA\nassertions compare id tuples so a failure cannot hang pytest on element\nreprs. mypy is clean on src.\n\nRefs #164\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Settle roundtrip futures on their own loop and hand runners over\n\nWith ipykernel 7 and a frontend that uses kernel subshells (JupyterLab\n4.5+, ipywidgets 8.1.8+), widget comm messages are handled on a subshell\nthread with its own event loop, while a runner scheduled from a cell\nlives on the kernel''s main loop. Two consequences surfaced in the\nNesting Plots acceptance test: the answer future was settled from the\nsubshell thread with a plain set_result, which is not thread-safe and\ndoes not wake the main loop, so each roundtrip of a cell-scheduled\nrunner completed only when the resend timer fired; and a click\nhandler''s refresh() reused that main-loop runner, so its await raised\n''attached to a different loop'' silently and the button never disabled.\nThe old cancel-and-recreate design hid both by always starting a fresh\ntask on the caller''s loop.\n\nutil.settle() applies set_result/set_exception through the future''s own\nloop with call_soon_threadsafe when called from another thread, and\nleaves an already-settled future alone. Pipe.schedule_run hands over\nwhen the alive runner belongs to a different loop: it cancels it (via\ncall_soon_threadsafe when needed; Pipeline.cancel re-records the taken\nflow) and starts a runner on the caller''s loop with the request count\npreserved.\n\nReproduced in a real browser with an instrumented notebook and in a\nheadless replay with a subshell thread; with the fix the acceptance\ntest shows master''s 0.63 s button profile and the replay''s roundtrip\ndrops from 1.1 s to 0.05 s.\n\nRefs #164 (item 3)\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Harden the Nesting Plots acceptance test, fix the harness, add changelog\n\nThe Nesting Plots test waited only for the toggle button to be enabled\nafter a click; if the disabling update had not reached the browser by\nthe first poll, the wait returned at once and the second click landed\nwhile the notebook''s own guard was still pending, so it was dropped.\nWait for the disabled state first (a 50 ms-poll keyword whose elapsed\ntime doubles as click-to-disabled latency), then for it to clear.\nMeasured on master and this branch: the button disables within a few\nmilliseconds on both.\n\nHarness: report bytes in both directions and the total (the previous\ncolumn was kernel-to-browser only, overstating the reduction); close\nevery widget and collect between cases so timings no longer drift with\ncase order (live objects across six cases 282k to 848k before, flat\nnow); footnote that the burst layout count is a lower bound without\n--slow-browser; add --cases for single-case runs.\n\nCHANGELOG: a 2.1.3 (unreleased) section covering the five #164 items,\nthe subshell fix, and the harness.\n\nRefs #164\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Pin the cross-loop roundtrip behaviour with a subshell-thread test\n\nA second thread runs its own event loop, standing in for the kernel\nsubshell that handles widget messages under ipykernel 7. The tests\ncheck that an answer written from that thread wakes a runner on the\nmain loop within a quarter of the resend interval, that a schedule_run\nfrom the other loop hands over to a runner on the caller''s loop with\nthe request count preserved and the taken flow re-recorded, and that a\ncancel from another thread lands on the runner''s loop. All three fail\nagainst the code before the fix.\n\nRefs #164\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Make the new tests pass on Python 3.10 and ipywidgets 8.0.1\n\nipywidgets 8.0.1 opens ipykernel''s Comm and only sends state when that\ncomm has a kernel, so with no kernel attached a kernel-side trait write\nnever reached the tests'' patched send. A conftest fixture attaches a\nkernel-bearing dummy comm on 8.0.x (a no-op on 8.1+, where the comm\npackage''s DummyComm already passes the check). On Python 3.10,\nwait_for resolves and cancellation settles one loop turn later than on\n3.12+, so the generation tests now drain turns until the state holds\ninstead of asserting after a fixed count. No source changes.\n\nRefs #164\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Trim inline comments; keep the rationale in short docstrings\n\nAddress review feedback on excessive code comments: multi-line inline\ncomments are folded into the docstrings of the functions they explain,\nand long docstrings and attribute comments are cut to what a reader\nneeds. No behaviour change.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_011vSpw2xTz8xqJoCPBrd7kw\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Dane <dane@Mac-Box.local>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"9481efd795104c4070a987d624121067ff90eee5","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyrdf/ipyelk/git/trees/9481efd795104c4070a987d624121067ff90eee5"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyrdf/ipyelk/git/commits/54fd52eb288f1eedf2c1e903900b6dde9855995c","verification":{"payload":"tree 9481efd795104c4070a987d624121067ff90eee5\nparent 0b415847777684ca1b0d39c04cd65470c415062b\nauthor dfreeman06 <freeman.dane@gmail.com> 1790813417 -0400\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790813417 -0400\n\n2.1.x substrate patch: echo off, take-at-start flow, generation counter, no inlet swap, linear validation (#168)\n\n* Add headless pipeline benchmark harness\n\nscripts/bench_pipeline.py runs a Diagram refresh without a browser, stubs\nthe text sizer (fixed width per character) and runs real elkjs in node,\nfeeds results back through the kernel''s receive path so echoes are\ncounted, and records wall time, per-stage time, comm messages by kind,\nbytes on the wire, layout runs, a collapse refresh, and a ten-refresh\nburst. scripts/bench_graphs.py generates deterministic flat and nested\ngraphs at 100/500/1000 nodes.\n\nSingle command: pixi run -e utest python scripts/bench_pipeline.py --label master\n\nRefs #164, #161, #167\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Stop echoing browser-written traits back to the frontends\n\nSelection.ids, Hover.ids and MarkElementWidget.value are written by the\nbrowser. With ipywidgets'' default echo_update the kernel re-sent each\nbrowser write to every frontend, and for value it then sent a second\nupdate carrying the re-serialised pydantic tree (which differs from the\nbrowser JSON by elkjs-internal keys), so every browser result arrived\nback twice and the viewer re-rendered on each. Tag the three traits\necho_update=False and, for value, skip the second send while the\nbrowser''s property lock is held. Kernel-initiated writes still send once.\n\nKnown limitation, documented on the traits: a second frontend attached\nto the same kernel no longer learns browser-made selections through the\necho (#161 rebroadcast question; a decision for the RenderBridge).\n\nRefs #164 (item 1), #161 defect 2\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Make edge-owner validation linear in the hierarchy\n\nElementIndex.check_edges asked networkx for the lowest common ancestor\nof every edge''s endpoints, and that call re-verifies the whole hierarchy\nis acyclic each time, so validation cost edges times nodes and ran twice\nper ValidationPipe.run. Compute depths once per check with a stack walk\nover children and find owners with a parent walk (ports resolve to their\nnode, self-loops to the node''s parent). ValidationPipe.apply_fixes now\nreports how many fixes it made and run() only re-reports on the outlet\nwhen something changed.\n\nMeasured on 10 groups with 3 root-appended edges per node:\n\n  nodes  edges  before    after\n    100    300  0.146 s   0.001 s\n    500   1500  3.533 s   0.005 s\n   1000   3000  14.420 s  0.010 s\n\nOwners are identical to networkx''s on seeded random trees (parametrized\ntest), including orphan adoption, port endpoints, and self-loops; the\npre-existing raise for a self-loop on a parentless node is preserved and\npinned.\n\nRefs #164 (item 5), #161 defect 1\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Take the pending flow at run start and stop swapping the inlet\n\nWriters recorded pending reports by assigning inlet.flow, and the\npipeline cleared it by assigning () when a run completed, so a report\nrecorded while a run was in flight (a tool that failed after mutating\nthe model, or a style change between completion and its callback) was\nerased by that completion. MarkElementWidget gains record(*tags), an\norder-preserving union, and take(), which returns the pending flow and\nleaves () behind. Pipeline.run takes at start and re-records what it\ntook if the run fails or is cancelled; Diagram, Tool.handler and\nToggleCollapsedTool record instead of assigning; Diagram.update_view no\nlonger clears the flow.\n\nDiagram.refresh also stopped swapping the browser''s laid-out copy into\npipe.inlet.value. The swap carried nothing that outlet.persist() does\nnot already merge onto the user''s objects through the shared index, and\non the next New run (a style change, for instance) validation rebuilt\nthe index from that copy, so selection resolved to copies and a hidden\nnode resolved to the slack Port sharing its id. ValidationPipe now\nrecognises a swapped-in merged copy and validates the indexed root.\n\nRefs #164 (items 2 and 4), #160 defect 3, #161\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Serve refresh requests with a generation counter instead of cancelling\n\nPipe.schedule_run cancelled the in-flight task and started another, so\na burst of refreshes cancelled runs the browser was already laying out,\na cancelled run''s browser answer could be consumed by its successor, and\nthe resend loop (every 0.5 to 2 s until answered) queued a duplicate\nlayout in the worker for every re-sent request.\n\nschedule_run now only counts a request. One runner task serves requests\non the trailing edge (ten calls in one tick cost one run; a call mid-run\ncosts one more run after it). A Pipeline gives way to a newer request at\nits next stage boundary (Superseded, taken flow re-recorded), and a\nbrowser roundtrip that was sent is always awaited. Pipe.cancel() is the\nexplicit way to stop a run; Diagram uses it only when its pipe or source\nis replaced. Each roundtrip carries a generation in the run message and\nthe frontend writes it back with its answer (MarkElementWidget.gen), so\nan answer for an abandoned generation is ignored and a re-sent request\nis recognised as the same work: the frontend RunQueue lays out once per\ngeneration and queues at most one newer request. Answers without a\ngeneration (an older extension build) are accepted with a one-time\nwarning.\n\nMeasured with the harness''s --slow-browser 0.6 at 100 nodes: layouts per\nrefresh 2 -> 1, wall 1.48 s -> 0.78 s. Immediate-answer path unchanged.\n\nRefs #164 (item 3), #161 defects 3 and 8\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Resolve roundtrips on the answer''s generation, not on a value change\n\nReview of the generation counter found a regression: the browser''s\noutlet update is Backbone''s deep-equal diff, so a re-run whose layout is\nidentical to what the frontend model already holds arrives as a change\nof gen alone (or as nothing at all on a third identical run), and\nwait_for_answer, which observed only value, waited out the 30 s\ntimeout. Before this branch the kernel''s re-serialised update after\nevery browser write kept the frontend value from ever being deep-equal,\nan accidental nonce that the echo suppression removed.\n\nwait_for_answer now observes value and gen and resolves when the\noutlet''s gen matches, with outlet.value as the result. The frontend\nanswers through one shared helper that silently clears value before the\nreal set so the tree is always in the diff too, for older kernels.\nError reports carry the failed request''s generation and the kernel\nrejects the pending roundtrip only for a matching generation (or when\nthe report has none). The gen-0 acceptance for older frontends now\nrequires the write to come from the browser (property lock held), so a\nkernel-side write during the first roundtrip is not taken for an answer.\nThe frontend RunQueue also ignores a re-sent request for a generation it\nalready completed, and the text sizer measures on a 250 ms timer when\nrequestAnimationFrame does not fire (background tab).\n\nThe harness emulates the frontend''s diff so this path is exercised.\n\nRefs #164 (item 3)\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Address review findings on the substrate patch\n\nEcho suppression: MarkElementWidget now suppresses the re-send only for\nthe very object the browser wrote (captured in set_trait while the\nproperty lock is held), so an observer that assigns a new tree during a\nbrowser write is still sent once.\n\nFlow: a superseded run that swallows its cancellation and completes late\nno longer resets the successor''s taken flow; a Pipeline nested as a\nsub-pipe receives the flow from its parent instead of re-taking from the\nshared inlet (and no longer re-records on failure, since the parent owes\nit); the ValidationPipe guard for a swapped-in browser copy is removed,\nsince nothing swaps the inlet any more and the guard could discard a\nuser''s edited tree.\n\nRunner: a cancelled roundtrip only clears the pipe''s roundtrip future if\nit still owns it (Python 3.10/3.11 unwind a turn late); the runner yields\nbetween runs so a request made from inside a synchronous run cannot spin\nthe loop; the older-frontend warning is per pipe; docstrings state that\non_error and status reflect the runner''s final outcome, that ownership\nvalidation trusts parent links, and that a source widget should feed one\npipeline.\n\nTests: record-not-assign is pinned at every call site (two tools mid-run,\nthe collapse tool, Diagram''s source flow); the late-unwind interleavings\nare reproduced with a pipe that catches CancelledError and yields; LCA\nassertions compare id tuples so a failure cannot hang pytest on element\nreprs. mypy is clean on src.\n\nRefs #164\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Settle roundtrip futures on their own loop and hand runners over\n\nWith ipykernel 7 and a frontend that uses kernel subshells (JupyterLab\n4.5+, ipywidgets 8.1.8+), widget comm messages are handled on a subshell\nthread with its own event loop, while a runner scheduled from a cell\nlives on the kernel''s main loop. Two consequences surfaced in the\nNesting Plots acceptance test: the answer future was settled from the\nsubshell thread with a plain set_result, which is not thread-safe and\ndoes not wake the main loop, so each roundtrip of a cell-scheduled\nrunner completed only when the resend timer fired; and a click\nhandler''s refresh() reused that main-loop runner, so its await raised\n''attached to a different loop'' silently and the button never disabled.\nThe old cancel-and-recreate design hid both by always starting a fresh\ntask on the caller''s loop.\n\nutil.settle() applies set_result/set_exception through the future''s own\nloop with call_soon_threadsafe when called from another thread, and\nleaves an already-settled future alone. Pipe.schedule_run hands over\nwhen the alive runner belongs to a different loop: it cancels it (via\ncall_soon_threadsafe when needed; Pipeline.cancel re-records the taken\nflow) and starts a runner on the caller''s loop with the request count\npreserved.\n\nReproduced in a real browser with an instrumented notebook and in a\nheadless replay with a subshell thread; with the fix the acceptance\ntest shows master''s 0.63 s button profile and the replay''s roundtrip\ndrops from 1.1 s to 0.05 s.\n\nRefs #164 (item 3)\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Harden the Nesting Plots acceptance test, fix the harness, add changelog\n\nThe Nesting Plots test waited only for the toggle button to be enabled\nafter a click; if the disabling update had not reached the browser by\nthe first poll, the wait returned at once and the second click landed\nwhile the notebook''s own guard was still pending, so it was dropped.\nWait for the disabled state first (a 50 ms-poll keyword whose elapsed\ntime doubles as click-to-disabled latency), then for it to clear.\nMeasured on master and this branch: the button disables within a few\nmilliseconds on both.\n\nHarness: report bytes in both directions and the total (the previous\ncolumn was kernel-to-browser only, overstating the reduction); close\nevery widget and collect between cases so timings no longer drift with\ncase order (live objects across six cases 282k to 848k before, flat\nnow); footnote that the burst layout count is a lower bound without\n--slow-browser; add --cases for single-case runs.\n\nCHANGELOG: a 2.1.3 (unreleased) section covering the five #164 items,\nthe subshell fix, and the harness.\n\nRefs #164\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Pin the cross-loop roundtrip behaviour with a subshell-thread test\n\nA second thread runs its own event loop, standing in for the kernel\nsubshell that handles widget messages under ipykernel 7. The tests\ncheck that an answer written from that thread wakes a runner on the\nmain loop within a quarter of the resend interval, that a schedule_run\nfrom the other loop hands over to a runner on the caller''s loop with\nthe request count preserved and the taken flow re-recorded, and that a\ncancel from another thread lands on the runner''s loop. All three fail\nagainst the code before the fix.\n\nRefs #164\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Make the new tests pass on Python 3.10 and ipywidgets 8.0.1\n\nipywidgets 8.0.1 opens ipykernel''s Comm and only sends state when that\ncomm has a kernel, so with no kernel attached a kernel-side trait write\nnever reached the tests'' patched send. A conftest fixture attaches a\nkernel-bearing dummy comm on 8.0.x (a no-op on 8.1+, where the comm\npackage''s DummyComm already passes the check). On Python 3.10,\nwait_for resolves and cancellation settles one loop turn later than on\n3.12+, so the generation tests now drain turns until the state holds\ninstead of asserting after a fixed count. No source changes.\n\nRefs #164\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Trim inline comments; keep the rationale in short docstrings\n\nAddress review feedback on excessive code comments: multi-line inline\ncomments are folded into the docstrings of the functions they explain,\nand long docstrings and attribute comments are cut to what a reader\nneeds. No behaviour change.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_011vSpw2xTz8xqJoCPBrd7kw\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Dane <dane@Mac-Box.local>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqvaTpCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAdaUQACGPNGIKrVORyA03DBGC6uh1\nz3i35VwnCaKw0BO8ezC8vMcY3hdt/oWmHunAkDj60gLvhvCGutSWCGaZwfv3GMGc\n6lozdu2W1OQmJGYIKvASNIFjVZfue/PBHA9RjWJujXOaUCDkXM+LKbXHYQY6VCOT\nntgNHjoLOZClNiYJL6j9jR3/QxKTlgrKlRmbyCcYBvXn2B/TQe8BfOjFbpaDGNRj\nDsctP7zvO5g/RuI06Jvl9RQ2PcmGSQaTsPQX/lYKoc6p+FWBuwFfKkbUr/7DYTbw\nPHTyAOBQuRcTc/t8O1hOyudE4h5kPdZ++Z7LCoUZLWWDYenWi0Df2grgHzkvRGro\n6kDDAinqObssywlFx5uE6v683I5tTn8sVpPsSU8TsYeSrAV03iNo9OppvExNom3d\n5ui6TTTh9R4XZqY5StPTBSr+DaWN5J8wiD38hH4/QSbLQH1KqPT3x2uOo38f8DFD\nega1NWlkDsS28FOl15BQt3MV+fvvzxvIvNQe8fuFneGjuc3PFCcE/LeT8gwntW+S\nFEx71i7c9S3a77hUONfAu1sVebLBxebN0tIk2vV8fcg8FAxvlNYxRGv6PhRBhhxe\noRV0sZM6Kz/82nk6zL6GAne6b8Elv/3EfP2KJhE51+6rw6UQPRr72bA5SqVEhXMu\nMDK5vAmD+H6xqzNyCrcU\n=5UVv\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-01T00:10:17Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/jupyrdf/ipyelk/commit/54fd52eb288f1eedf2c1e903900b6dde9855995c","node_id":"C_kwDOEQFCU9oAKDU0ZmQ1MmViMjg4ZjFlZWRmMmMxZTkwMzkwMGI2ZGRlOTg1NTk5NWM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/jupyrdf/ipyelk/commit/0b415847777684ca1b0d39c04cd65470c415062b","sha":"0b415847777684ca1b0d39c04cd65470c415062b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyrdf/ipyelk/commits/0b415847777684ca1b0d39c04cd65470c415062b"}],"sha":"54fd52eb288f1eedf2c1e903900b6dde9855995c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyrdf/ipyelk/commits/54fd52eb288f1eedf2c1e903900b6dde9855995c"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/6493354?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dfreeman06/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dfreeman06/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dfreeman06/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dfreeman06/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/dfreeman06","id":6493354,"login":"dfreeman06","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY0OTMzNTQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dfreeman06/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dfreeman06/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dfreeman06/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dfreeman06/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dfreeman06/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dfreeman06","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyrdf/ipyelk/commits/0b415847777684ca1b0d39c04cd65470c415062b/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-30T00:30:05Z","email":"freeman.dane@gmail.com","name":"dfreeman06"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-30T00:30:05Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"2.1.x fixes, part 2: hidden-node projection crash, null and port ids, nested label spacing, first-frame culling and export viewBox (#172)\n\n* Never serialise a null id, and compose port ids from the parent''s id\n\nOutside a Registry block an element without an explicit id serialised\nas null, so an edge went on the wire as sources: [null], and a port\nunder Node(id=\"N\") got <uuid>.<uuid> because the port asked the\nregistry for its parent instead of using the parent''s own id.\n\nElements now mint a private wire id once, on first serialisation, and\nuse it for their own id and for edge endpoints; the explicit id trait\nstays None so validation still reports and fixes unassigned ids. The\nregistry seeds from that wire id, so the id first seen on the wire is\nthe id later stamped by build_index, apply_fixes, and NXLoader: one id\nfor the object''s life. Port ids compose from the parent''s id in and out\nof a registry. Label.wrap no longer copies a generated id onto every\nwrapped line.\n\nRefs #169, #160 defects 1 and 2\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Project hidden elements onto their visible ancestor, not a sibling\n\niter_visible reassigned its loop-carried hidden and last_visible\nvariables inside the sibling loop, so once one sibling was hidden every\nlater sibling was reported hidden and a hidden node''s slack port landed\non its preceding sibling. For a hidden first child of the root the\nslack port landed on the root itself, which elkjs rejects with a null\ndereference (the crash the benchmark harness worked around by hiding\nincident edges).\n\nEach element''s visibility is now computed per element and the nearest\nvisible ancestor is carried down, so a hidden compound child projects\nonto the compound as the collapse tool expects. An edge whose visible\nprojection would touch the root is dropped from the wire instead of\nproducing a root port; the Edge object stays in the index and returns\nunchanged when the node is revealed.\n\nHarness collapse rows on flat graphs now lay out (100: 107 ms, 500:\n751 ms, 1000: 2.20 s) where master raised.\n\nRefs #169, #161 defect 10\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Add nested label spacing regardless of the sublabel width\n\nsize_nested_label computed width += ls.width or 0 + spacing, which adds\nthe spacing only when the sublabel width is falsy. Parenthesise the\nfallback. The function has no caller in src today; the docstring says\nso.\n\nRefs #169, #161 defect 8\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Fix the first-frame culling bug, keep culling off, size exports from the model\n\nvalidCanvasBounds returned width == 0 && height == 0, so the culling\nguard in the node and edge views never culled on a sized canvas and\nculled almost everything on the 0x0 canvas that exists before the first\nInitializeCanvasBoundsAction: the inverse of its comment. The guard now\nskips culling while the canvas size is unknown, and culling stays off\nbehind an explicit CULLING_ENABLED constant. Turning it on is #170:\non the normal path the diagram is fit on layout, so culling removes\nnothing and adds a bounds walk per element, while it makes every\nconsumer of the live SVG (the exporter, the acceptance suites, user\ncode) viewport-dependent; that needs a measurement and an export design\nfirst.\n\nThe SVG exporter sized its viewBox from the live group''s bounding rect,\nwhich under-sized by the layout''s left margin. It now uses the model''s\ncontent extent, a recursive union of every element''s bounds in diagram\ncoordinates (edge labels, outside-placed node labels, overhanging ports\nincluded), so the export does not depend on the viewport. The exporter\nacceptance suite scrolls the diagram out of sight, re-exports from the\nkernel, and asserts node, edge, and label counts plus a positive\nviewBox.\n\nRefs #169, #161 defect 5, #170\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Give copies their own wire id, adopt registry-minted ids, add changelog\n\nReview of the wire-id change found two gaps. A copied element carried\nits private wire id, so a deep-copied id-less node shared an id with\nits original and one of them silently lost its place in the index; the\ncopy hooks now reset the wire id while an explicit id is copied as\nbefore. And an element indexed inside a Registry before it was ever\nserialised minted a second id on the wire; a registry-minted id is now\nadopted as the wire id, so the first id an object gets anywhere is the\none it keeps.\n\nCHANGELOG gains a 2.1.3 (unreleased) section for the four items.\n\nRefs #169\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Dane <dane@Mac-Box.local>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"d644444ee1b2e3860e628d7a30e04874e564a097","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyrdf/ipyelk/git/trees/d644444ee1b2e3860e628d7a30e04874e564a097"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyrdf/ipyelk/git/commits/0b415847777684ca1b0d39c04cd65470c415062b","verification":{"payload":"tree d644444ee1b2e3860e628d7a30e04874e564a097\nparent 7570e6cc2111e4a2adff748bb4047d8084d1496d\nauthor dfreeman06 <freeman.dane@gmail.com> 1790728205 -0400\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790728205 -0400\n\n2.1.x fixes, part 2: hidden-node projection crash, null and port ids, nested label spacing, first-frame culling and export viewBox (#172)\n\n* Never serialise a null id, and compose port ids from the parent''s id\n\nOutside a Registry block an element without an explicit id serialised\nas null, so an edge went on the wire as sources: [null], and a port\nunder Node(id=\"N\") got <uuid>.<uuid> because the port asked the\nregistry for its parent instead of using the parent''s own id.\n\nElements now mint a private wire id once, on first serialisation, and\nuse it for their own id and for edge endpoints; the explicit id trait\nstays None so validation still reports and fixes unassigned ids. The\nregistry seeds from that wire id, so the id first seen on the wire is\nthe id later stamped by build_index, apply_fixes, and NXLoader: one id\nfor the object''s life. Port ids compose from the parent''s id in and out\nof a registry. Label.wrap no longer copies a generated id onto every\nwrapped line.\n\nRefs #169, #160 defects 1 and 2\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Project hidden elements onto their visible ancestor, not a sibling\n\niter_visible reassigned its loop-carried hidden and last_visible\nvariables inside the sibling loop, so once one sibling was hidden every\nlater sibling was reported hidden and a hidden node''s slack port landed\non its preceding sibling. For a hidden first child of the root the\nslack port landed on the root itself, which elkjs rejects with a null\ndereference (the crash the benchmark harness worked around by hiding\nincident edges).\n\nEach element''s visibility is now computed per element and the nearest\nvisible ancestor is carried down, so a hidden compound child projects\nonto the compound as the collapse tool expects. An edge whose visible\nprojection would touch the root is dropped from the wire instead of\nproducing a root port; the Edge object stays in the index and returns\nunchanged when the node is revealed.\n\nHarness collapse rows on flat graphs now lay out (100: 107 ms, 500:\n751 ms, 1000: 2.20 s) where master raised.\n\nRefs #169, #161 defect 10\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Add nested label spacing regardless of the sublabel width\n\nsize_nested_label computed width += ls.width or 0 + spacing, which adds\nthe spacing only when the sublabel width is falsy. Parenthesise the\nfallback. The function has no caller in src today; the docstring says\nso.\n\nRefs #169, #161 defect 8\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Fix the first-frame culling bug, keep culling off, size exports from the model\n\nvalidCanvasBounds returned width == 0 && height == 0, so the culling\nguard in the node and edge views never culled on a sized canvas and\nculled almost everything on the 0x0 canvas that exists before the first\nInitializeCanvasBoundsAction: the inverse of its comment. The guard now\nskips culling while the canvas size is unknown, and culling stays off\nbehind an explicit CULLING_ENABLED constant. Turning it on is #170:\non the normal path the diagram is fit on layout, so culling removes\nnothing and adds a bounds walk per element, while it makes every\nconsumer of the live SVG (the exporter, the acceptance suites, user\ncode) viewport-dependent; that needs a measurement and an export design\nfirst.\n\nThe SVG exporter sized its viewBox from the live group''s bounding rect,\nwhich under-sized by the layout''s left margin. It now uses the model''s\ncontent extent, a recursive union of every element''s bounds in diagram\ncoordinates (edge labels, outside-placed node labels, overhanging ports\nincluded), so the export does not depend on the viewport. The exporter\nacceptance suite scrolls the diagram out of sight, re-exports from the\nkernel, and asserts node, edge, and label counts plus a positive\nviewBox.\n\nRefs #169, #161 defect 5, #170\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n* Give copies their own wire id, adopt registry-minted ids, add changelog\n\nReview of the wire-id change found two gaps. A copied element carried\nits private wire id, so a deep-copied id-less node shared an id with\nits original and one of them silently lost its place in the index; the\ncopy hooks now reset the wire id while an explicit id is copied as\nbefore. And an element indexed inside a Registry before it was ever\nserialised minted a second id on the wire; a registry-minted id is now\nadopted as the wire id, so the first id an object gets anywhere is the\none it keeps.\n\nCHANGELOG gains a 2.1.3 (unreleased) section for the four items.\n\nRefs #169\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Kxt4rZqPBsuFpEfvXiVRdQ\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Dane <dane@Mac-Box.local>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqvFgNCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAvQkQAIaPEHp8UTljKgdYqmuZxNAR\nzZAjKwa4wAjW9GTAhcqQ/wRtuMapZ3rJkpmVY1sDu3lnIpCJQor7dMgFfR8QBv1Y\noftr3lHWjO9iezSGme6VDiXN3joJ1XVAu3/BeWEZnvvx5E4IcVpiRfCvsfVZakmv\nBYFzxXiL3RCp0wOiNZa184NW1Vg+gaG7/hTx2zscbFa7fReDH2ULISQ4RIlf/GM7\netWXIPx/smNSkwGaoruNjIUdgNrtxJ7HpRBv0if4SqYM